---

<DocShell
   title="Run an audit"
   summary="a1 audit runs a WCAG audit of a website, a page, a feature, or a mobile app with AI agents doing the testing. It splits the work into small units that agents test in parallel, stores every result as it arrives, shows progress while it runs, and writes a report you can hand to a client."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="how">
      <h2>How an audit works</h2>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Page</strong><span>One thing to test: a page, a screen, a state of one such as an open dialog, or a flow such as checkout.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Test pass</strong><span>The criteria tested with one kind of interaction: <code>semantics</code>, <code>keyboard</code>, <code>visual</code>, <code>media</code>, <code>forms</code>, <code>pointer</code>, and <code>consistency</code> across pages. A page is loaded and exercised once per pass, not once per criterion.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Unit</strong><span>One pass on one page, such as <code>P3.keyboard</code>. A worker claims a unit, tests it, and submits an outcome for each of its criteria.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Finding</strong><span>One defect. The same defect on ten pages is one finding with ten occurrences.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         Everything lives in <code>.a11ied/audits/&lt;id&gt;/</code>: a SQLite database, an
         <code>evidence/</code> folder of screenshots, and the report. An audit survives a
         crashed agent, a closed terminal, or a restart, and any number of workers can use it
         at once.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="start">
      <h2>Start one</h2>
      <p>
         The quickest way is to ask an agent that has the
         <a href="/guides/agent-skill"><code>a11ied-audit</code> skill</a>:
      </p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Audit https://example.com against WCAG 2.2 AA with a11ied.
Do not submit the contact form. Use four workers.</code></pre>
      <p>The agent runs these commands. You can run them yourself:</p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit init https://example.com --rule "Do not submit the contact form"
a1 audit discover          # add the site's pages to the audit
a1 audit scan              # automated checks on every page, in parallel
a1 audit status            # 0 of N units done</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>--rule</code> states something workers must not do. Every work packet repeats the
         rules. <code>--access</code> says how to reach the target, such as where the test
         account is.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="scope">
      <h2>Choose the scope</h2>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><strong>A whole site</strong><span><code>a1 audit init &lt;url&gt;</code>, then <code>a1 audit discover</code>. Remove what is out of scope with <code>a1 audit page exclude &lt;id&gt; --reason "..."</code>. On a large site keep one page per template plus the pages in key tasks.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>One page</strong><span><code>a1 audit init &lt;url&gt; --page</code></span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>A state or a flow</strong><span><code>a1 audit page add &lt;url&gt; --kind state --name "Menu open" --notes "Activate the Menu button"</code>. Use <code>--kind flow</code> with the steps in <code>--notes</code> for a process that spans pages.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Chosen criteria</strong><span><code>--criteria 1.4.3,2.4.7</code> audits only those. Only the passes that own them get units.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>One kind of testing</strong><span><code>--passes keyboard,forms</code></span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Another level or version</strong><span><code>--level A</code>, <code>--level AAA</code>, <code>--wcag 2.1</code>. The default is WCAG 2.2 Level AA: 55 criteria.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         The criteria that compare pages, such as 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation, need at least two
         pages. In a one-page audit the report lists them as not evaluated.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="split">
      <h2>What is automated and what is judged</h2>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Automated</strong><span>Finding pages. axe-core failures, which become findings directly. Measurements stored as signals: tab order, focus styles, overflow at 320 pixels and 200 percent zoom, target sizes, media and form inventories. Claims, recovery, progress, and assembling the report.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Judged by a worker</strong><span>Every pass and every not-applicable. Whether an automated finding is real on this page. Everything a scanner cannot see: whether alt text describes the image, whether focus order makes sense, whether an error message helps. Severity, impact, and the fix.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         The scan never passes a criterion. Signals appear in a worker's packet as facts to
         check, which saves the worker from collecting them, and nothing else.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="workers">
      <h2>Run the workers</h2>
      <p>
         A worker is an agent in a loop: claim a unit, test it, submit, repeat. Start several
         with different names and they take different units.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit next --worker w1
a1 audit submit P1.keyboard --worker w1 --file result.json</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>next</code> prints the work packet: the page, the rules, the procedure, each
         criterion with what it applies to and when it fails, the scan's signals, findings
         already recorded, the page's states to cover, and a submission template. A finding on
         a web page gives a selector for its element and the state to put the page in, and
         <code>submit</code> takes the outlined screenshot itself. <code>submit</code> takes
         one JSON document and accepts or rejects it whole. A rejection lists every problem, such as a
         criterion with no outcome or a failure with no finding.
      </p>
      <p>
         A native screen reader and a physical device can serve one worker at a time. Give one
         worker <code>--pass semantics</code> and the rest <code>--not-pass semantics</code>
         when the audit uses VoiceOver or NVDA. The simulated reader runs in parallel.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="track">
      <h2>Track progress</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 audit status       # summary: units, workers, blocks, findings, recent activity
a1 audit status --units
a1 audit log --unit P3.forms
a1 audit dashboard    # live page on localhost, refreshes every five seconds
a1 audit findings</code></pre>
      <p>
         The dashboard shows a grid of pages by test pass, which worker holds what, how long
         each has been working, what is blocked and why, the findings so far, and the activity
         log. It links to a live interim report.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="stuck">
      <h2>When something is stuck</h2>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><strong>A worker died</strong><span>Nothing is lost. Its claim expires after 20 minutes without activity and the unit returns to the queue. A worker that asks for work under the same name gets its unit back.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>A unit is blocked</strong><span>A worker blocks a unit it cannot test at all, with the reason. Other units carry on. Fix the cause, then <code>a1 audit reopen &lt;unit&gt; --reason "..."</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>One check cannot be done</strong><span>The worker submits the unit and records that criterion as <code>cannot-tell</code> with the reason. A rule against sending a form still lets workers test its validation and error messages; only what lies past the forbidden effect, such as the success message, is <code>cannot-tell</code>. The report lists it under Not verified.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>A state could not be reached</strong><span>The scan lists the controls on each page that open a menu, dialog, widget, tab, or section, and every submission must account for each one. A state a worker could not reach is recorded as not tested with the reason, and the report lists it under Not verified.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>A tool or device is missing</strong><span>When something affects the whole audit, such as no screen reader or no physical phone, record it with <code>a1 audit limitation "..." --criterion 4.1.2</code>. The report lists it under Not verified, and a reader can see which passes rest on weaker testing.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>A unit keeps failing</strong><span>After three abandoned attempts the unit blocks itself. Read <code>a1 audit log --unit &lt;id&gt;</code> to see why.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>A result looks wrong</strong><span><code>a1 audit reopen &lt;unit&gt;</code> queues it again, and the next worker sees its earlier outcomes and the reason. <code>a1 audit finding dismiss</code>, <code>merge</code>, and <code>update</code> correct findings.</span></div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="report">
      <h2>The report</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 audit report</code></pre>
      <p>
         It writes <code>audit-report.html</code>, <code>audit-report.md</code>, and
         <code>audit-report.json</code> to the audit's <code>report/</code> folder. The HTML
         file is self-contained with screenshots embedded; print it from a browser for PDF.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Summary</strong><span>One statement of the result, findings by severity, and criteria by outcome.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>What to fix first</strong><span>Findings ordered by severity, then by how many pages they affect.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Findings</strong><span>Each with the problem, who it affects, how to fix it, where it occurs, and a cropped screenshot of the failing element with an outline around it. Any screenshot opens in a viewer; one too tall for its thumbnail fades out at the bottom with a button to see all of it.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Results by success criterion</strong><span>Fails, Passes, Not applicable, Not verified, or Not evaluated for every criterion in scope.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Not verified</strong><span>What could not be established, and why.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Scope and method</strong><span>The pages audited and the states tested on each, what was excluded, how the testing was done, and each decision's note for readers who want to check the work.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         A report built before every unit is done is marked interim and says what is
         unfinished. A report never states conformance while a criterion is failing, unfinished,
         or not verified.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="mobile">
      <h2>Mobile apps</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 audit init "Acme iOS 4.2" --platform ios --access "Simulator: iPhone 16"
a1 audit page add "Tab bar > Search" --name "Search"</code></pre>
      <p>
         Tracking, parallel workers, findings, and the report work the same way. There is no
         automated scan, and a1 does not drive the app: workers operate it with the
         platform's tools, such as <code>xcrun simctl</code>, <code>adb</code>, or an automation
         server the agent already has. Give each worker its own simulator or device. Work
         packets use the mobile wording of each procedure.
      </p>
      <p>
         On Android, <code>a1 talkback on</code> and <code>a1 talkback walk --count 20</code>
         print what TalkBack said at each focus stop. It needs a phone or a Google Play
         emulator image, and TalkBack's log output level set to verbose once per device. See
         <a href="/reference/cli#talkback"><code>a1 talkback</code></a>.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
